{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg73EDt7kaQXxWpGZzJ3u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Firecrawl caches scrapes by default, and you can bypass the cache with `maxAge`. I'm answering from memory of the docs and haven't checked them just now.\n\n**Cache duration:** the default `maxAge` is 2 days (172,800,000 ms). If Firecrawl has a cached copy of the page younger than that, it returns the copy instead of re-scraping.\n\n**Forcing a fresh scrape:** set `maxAge` to `0`.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST https://api.firecrawl.dev/v2/scrape \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $FIRECRAWL_API_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"url\": \"https://example.com\", \"maxAge\": 0}'\n```\n\nSDK equivalents:\n- **Python:** `app.scrape(\"https://example.com\", max_age=0)`\n- **JS:** `app.scrape(\"https://example.com\", { maxAge: 0 })`\n\n**Other options:**\n- **A middle ground:** set `maxAge` to the staleness you can tolerate, in ms. For example, `3600000` accepts anything up to 1 hour old. Cached hits are much faster, so use `0` only where you need it.\n- **Skip writing to the cache:** `storeInCache: false` stops that scrape from being cached.\n- **Crawl, batch scrape and search:** these take the same setting through their scrape options. In a crawl it goes under `scrapeOptions`, so check that you set it there and not only at the top level.\n- **Other causes of stale content:** if `maxAge: 0` still gives old content, the page itself may be serving stale content, for example through a CDN or JavaScript-rendered data. Adding `waitFor` or checking the raw page can help.\n\nI can look up the current docs or your CLI version to confirm the parameter names. If you share the call you're making, I can check that too."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6786,"cache_read_input_tokens":11227,"cache_creat